# LOCK — environment
#
# Copy to .env.local and fill in from your Supabase project
# (Project Settings → API). .env.local is git-ignored; never commit it.

# ── Supabase ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both values are safe in the browser. The anon key grants exactly what Row
# Level Security allows and nothing more, and every table in this schema has
# RLS enabled with no permissive policy anywhere.
#
# The URL is the project's base origin and nothing else — no path. The client
# appends /auth/v1, /rest/v1 and /storage/v1 itself, so pasting the RESTful
# endpoint (…supabase.co/rest/v1) that sits beside it in the dashboard sends
# every sign-in to …/rest/v1/auth/v1/authorize and fails with "No API key found
# in request". `src/lib/env.ts` trims that suffix back, but set it correctly.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ── Site ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The canonical origin. Used for absolute metadata URLs and, more importantly,
# for the email confirmation link's redirect target — which must also be listed
# in Supabase → Authentication → URL Configuration → Redirect URLs.
#
# Optional locally, where the localhost default is correct. Set it on every
# deployment: the server can fall back to VERCEL_URL, but the browser cannot,
# because VERCEL_URL is not a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable.
#
# No trailing slash. Include the scheme.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# ── Google sign-in needs nothing here ───────────────────────────────────────
# The Google client ID and secret live in the Supabase dashboard
# (Authentication → Sign In / Providers → Google), not in this file and not in
# this repository. LOCK never reads them: it asks Supabase for the authorization
# URL, and Supabase holds the secret. See SETUP.md section 2b.

# ── Deliberately absent ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY is not read anywhere in this codebase.
#
# The service-role key bypasses Row Level Security entirely, so it is a key
# worth introducing only when a specific job cannot be done any other way —
# writing rows no user is allowed to write, for example. Foundation has no such
# job: every read and write here happens as the signed-in user, through RLS.
#
# When a later phase needs it, it belongs in exactly one server-only module,
# never with a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, and the reason belongs in ARCHITECTURE.md.
